<bx:include template="../partials/_head.bxm">
<bx:output>
<h1>Process Lifecycle</h1>
<p class="bx-docs-lede">What actually happens on startup, shutdown, a port conflict, and a dev-mode file change.</p>

<h2>Startup: listen() blocks the process</h2>
<p><code class="bx-inline">app.listen()</code> blocks the calling thread by default (<code class="bx-inline">options.block = true</code>). Node keeps a CLI process alive via its event loop; BoxLang's CLI runtime has no equivalent, so <code class="bx-inline">listen()</code> blocks itself rather than requiring every caller to remember a keep-alive loop:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.listen( 3000, ( port ) => println( "listening on ##port##" ) )
// process stays alive here until app.close() is called or the process is signaled</code></pre>
<p>Pass <code class="bx-inline">{ block: false }</code> for non-blocking startup — useful for a test suite, or embedding the server inside a larger app that manages its own lifecycle. <code class="bx-inline">app.close()</code> stops the server either way.</p>

<h2>Graceful shutdown (Ctrl-C / SIGTERM)</h2>
<p>The server registers a JVM shutdown hook when it starts, so <code class="bx-inline">Ctrl-C</code> or <code class="bx-inline">SIGTERM</code> always shuts down cleanly rather than just dropping requests: it runs <code class="bx-inline">app.shutdown()</code>, which stops accepting new requests, closes open WebSocket connections, and gives requests already running up to <code class="bx-inline">shutdownTimeoutMs</code> (default 5 seconds) to finish before the listening socket is released and the dev-mode file watcher stopped.</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "shutdownTimeoutMs", 15000 )            // wait longer for slow requests
app.shutdown( { timeoutMs: 15000, drainDelayMs: 3000 } )    // or stop on demand</code></pre>
<p><code class="bx-inline">drainDelayMs</code> keeps serving normally for that long while readiness already reports "draining" — time for a load balancer to notice and stop routing to you before requests start being refused. <code class="bx-inline">app.shutdown()</code> returns <code class="bx-inline">{ drained, webSocketsClosed }</code>; <code class="bx-inline">drained</code> is <code class="bx-inline">false</code> if the timeout ran out with requests still running. A long-lived response such as an SSE stream counts as running until it ends, so it will use the whole timeout. Set <code class="bx-inline">shutdownTimeoutMs</code> to <code class="bx-inline">0</code> for the old immediate stop, which is also what <code class="bx-inline">app.close()</code> does; both are safe to call more than once.</p>

<h2>Health checks</h2>
<p><code class="bx-inline">app.health()</code> adds two endpoints for load balancers and orchestrators — this site has them on: <a href="/health/live">/health/live</a> (200 as long as the process can answer at all) and <a href="/health/ready">/health/ready</a> (200 when the app should receive traffic, 503 when it shouldn't).</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.health( { checks: { database: () => pingDatabase() } } )   // before app.use( ... ) if probes shouldn't create sessions</code></pre>
<p>A check returns <code class="bx-inline">false</code>, or throws, when its dependency is unhealthy; readiness then answers <code class="bx-inline">503</code> naming the failing checks — the names only, never the exception text, which is logged instead. Readiness also answers <code class="bx-inline">503 { "status": "draining" }</code> the moment <code class="bx-inline">app.shutdown()</code> begins. In Kubernetes, point <code class="bx-inline">readinessProbe</code> at the ready path and <code class="bx-inline">livenessProbe</code> at the live one, and keep <code class="bx-inline">terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> above your <code class="bx-inline">shutdownTimeoutMs</code>.</p>
<div class="bx-callout">
  <strong>Why this exists</strong>
  Without the shutdown hook, the JVM could tear the process down without ever running cleanup — the process would exit, but the port could be left in a state where restarting the server immediately afterward failed, and the dev-mode watcher thread wasn't guaranteed to stop cleanly either. Draining first is what stops a deploy from cutting off the requests that were mid-flight.
</div>

<h2>Port already in use</h2>
<p>If the requested port is already bound, the server exits with a short, readable message instead of a raw Java stack trace:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">plain — stdout</span><code>[BoxExpress] Port 3000 is already in use — exiting.</code></pre>
<p>The process exits with status code <code class="bx-inline">1</code>. This is detected by inspecting the actual <code class="bx-inline">java.net.BindException</code> cause (not by string-matching the error message), so it's specific to a real port conflict — any other startup failure still surfaces normally.</p>

<h2>Dev-mode auto-reload</h2>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "reloadOnChange", true )</code></pre>
<p>This restarts the app whenever a <code class="bx-inline">.bx</code> or <code class="bx-inline">.bxs</code> file changes under the entry script's directory (recursively, skipping dot-directories and <code class="bx-inline">node_modules</code>/<code class="bx-inline">boxlang_modules</code>/<code class="bx-inline">target</code>/<code class="bx-inline">build</code>/<code class="bx-inline">dist</code>). <code class="bx-inline">.bxm</code> views aren't watched: <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> re-reads a template from disk on every request, so a view edit shows up on the next request with no restart at all.</p>
<p>BoxLang can't reload compiled classes inside a running JVM, so since BoxLang Express 0.2.22 the process you start becomes a <strong>supervisor</strong> and your app runs in a <strong>child process</strong>:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Your script runs up to <code class="bx-inline">app.listen()</code>. There, in the supervisor, <code class="bx-inline">listen()</code> doesn't bind the port: it starts the child with the same command line and starts watching files.</li>
  <li>The child runs your script from the top and serves requests.</li>
  <li>On a change, <code class="bx-inline">[reloadOnChange] &lt;path&gt; changed — restarting...</code> is logged; the supervisor stops the child with <code class="bx-inline">SIGTERM</code>, waits for it to exit, and starts a new one. The old server has always released the port before the new one binds it.</li>
  <li>If the child dies on its own (say you saved a syntax error), <code class="bx-inline">[reloadOnChange] app exited with code 1 — waiting for a file change to restart.</code> is logged, and the next save brings it back.</li>
</ol>
<p>Because the supervisor is the process your shell, IDE or container started and it stays up for the whole session, <code class="bx-inline">Ctrl-C</code> stops everything, <code class="bx-inline">SIGTERM</code> to it stops the child first, and if it's killed outright the child notices within a second and exits. In a container it can be PID 1 and the container keeps running across restarts.</p>
<div class="bx-callout">
  <strong>What runs where</strong>
  Code before <code class="bx-inline">app.listen()</code> runs in both processes. Registering routes and middleware there is harmless; <code class="bx-inline">app.schedule()</code> jobs and the cluster manager are shut down in the supervisor so they only run in the child. The <code class="bx-inline">listen()</code> callback, and anything after <code class="bx-inline">listen()</code>, runs only in the child. Dev mode runs two JVMs, so expect roughly double the memory.
</div>

<h2>Using an external watcher instead</h2>
<p>Any restart tool that runs your app as its own child works unchanged. Leave <code class="bx-inline">reloadOnChange</code> off and let the tool watch:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>watchexec -r -e bx,bxs -- boxlang app.bxs
nodemon -e bx,bxs --exec boxlang app.bxs</code></pre>
<p>These give finer control over what's watched, use one JVM instead of two, and run your script once per restart. Their <code class="bx-inline">SIGTERM</code> goes through the normal graceful shutdown above.</p>

<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Before 0.2.22</strong>
  Older versions restarted by launching a replacement process and exiting the original. Ctrl-C then no longer reached the running server (stop it with <code class="bx-inline">kill -TERM &lt;pid&gt;</code>), a supervisor saw the original exit, a container whose PID 1 was the original stopped outright, and a startup error in the saved code left nothing watching for the fix. Upgrade, or use an external watcher.
</div>

